about our product overview.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:06.710
So what is Anthos Migrate?

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:08.910
How does it work?

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:11.880
And then we'll move
further on the next topic.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:15.140
So first, I'd like to ask
in the room, how many of you

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:16.730
know Kubernetes?

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:18.170
Can I get a raise of hand?

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:18.780
Majority.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:19.280
OK.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:20.300
Great.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:23.440
So we'll go quickly
over this one.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:26.550
Portable, open-source
management platform,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:29.660
allowing you to scale,
to run, basically

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:34.370
to orchestrate your containers
and to run them with policy,

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:40.160
declarative model, and be able
to run on various clusters,

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:41.430
various environments.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:45.500
So initially, our
internal systems obviously

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.530
run on Kubernetes,
and now we do it

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:52.160
with managed to Kubernetes,
GKE, where we actually

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.860
take care of managing
the kernel for you,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.800
providing you upgrades,
security patches, fixes.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:03.530
And so our SRE teams operate
the management of the kernel

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.420
and OS for you
when you are on GKE

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:08.240
versus running
Kubernetes by yourself

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.520
on an open-source model.

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:14.790
So as we know GKE,
I'll move forward.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:16.820
So at this point,
we're at a place where

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.560
you have applications,
hopefully, that

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:22.820
are VMs in a data center,
you know the value of GKE,

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:24.393
and you want to
modernize these apps

00:10:24.393 --> 00:10:26.060
or you want these
apps to be able to run

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.300
in that new modern environment.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:29.300
So then, what do you do?

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:32.380
Then comes this
product, Anthos Migrate.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:33.650
OK.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:38.260
And here is, at a
glance, what we do.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:40.670
You start-- and the
way to read the slide,

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:42.590
we'll go on the left-hand
side of the slide.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:45.200
I'd like you to read
it from bottom, up.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:45.890
OK?

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:47.870
So at the bottom,
what you see is

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:50.450
that we have different
sources of environments.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:54.720
You can be on premise, you
can be on a public cloud,

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:58.840
and you have, as a starting
point, a virtual machine.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:02.900
Your destination is
up on this slide,

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:04.960
which is the GCP platform.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.840
And where you end
up is basically

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:10.990
on containers running
on GKE, and then you

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:14.920
have other services-- service
mesh-- that come and attach

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:15.870
themselves to that.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:19.090
So all the ecosystem
of the GCP platform

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:23.080
come, and then can interact
with your GKE cluster

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:24.610
once you've done that move.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:25.210
OK.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:27.130
So we can do VMware.

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:29.090
We can do physical bare metal.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:32.710
We can do other cloud
type of migration,

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:34.990
and the source is
a virtual machine.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:42.770
Here is how this works.

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:46.950
And for you to understand
Anthos Migrate,

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.790
there's two things that
you need to understand.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:53.120
One is understand GKE
and understand GCP.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:54.410
And this is new.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:57.950
This can be new when you
are in an enterprise or a VM

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.360
environment and you're
moving to the cloud.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.860
So one aspect to know
is containers and GKE

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:05.490
and how GKE works.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.930
And the second part is the
Anthos Migrate Velostrata

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:10.200
technology.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:13.090
And so we intersect
at these two.

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:15.140
And the way we've
designed this is

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:19.070
to give you the journey and
experience of Kubernetes.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:21.020
So how do we start the journey?

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:25.250
You're a user, and
you're over here,

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.980
and you want to
go on containers.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.430
So the way you will
do that is basically,

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:33.380
you're going to do
a deployment policy.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:36.620
You will have a YAML
file, which is Kubernetes,

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:41.340
and you will tell GKE that
you want to start a migration.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:43.550
So actually, you
start your migration

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:49.650
by talking to GKE versus going
on your on-premise environment.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:54.110
And so you push the YAML file,
and then what GKE will do,

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:59.250
GKE integrates with our
Anthos Migrate technology.

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:02.000
And then we start the
migration of the VM.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.250
So you have these
VMs that you see here

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.440
that are running in
your data center,

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.170
and we've received from
Kubernetes, from GKE,

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:12.050
we received the
instructions to migrate,

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.780
and then Velostrata Anthos
Migrate starts the migration.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:17.840
So you have these
two blue VMs that

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:24.650
are running on your data center,
and where we move is over here.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:26.930
You end up with two containers.

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:29.030
So I did a one-on-one
mapping, just to show you

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:32.960
the example, the visual example
where you have two VMs running

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:39.260
on premise, and the end result
is two containers running

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:40.920
on GKE.

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.650
And it's all
orchestrated by GKE.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:49.100
And so that's really
important concept to know.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:51.530
Now, with that, I'll tell
you a little bit more

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:55.200
under the hood in terms of
how we enable this migration.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:58.020
So I'll just move to this slide.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:04.520
We have, basically, here,
our Velostrata Anthos Migrate

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:08.370
technology resides
into two places.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:13.190
One is on premise, we have
a Velostrata appliance,

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.930
which is a VM, running
on one of your vCenter

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:18.350
which is basically our back end.

00:14:18.350 --> 00:14:21.620
And we have an ESX plug-in,
so a plug-in for VMware

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:24.880
that will basically give
you the manageability

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:29.370
and the interaction with the
virtual machines over there.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.530
So that's one part
of the deployment.

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:35.250
So you install that on your
on-premise environment.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:41.300
Number two, on the cloud we have
running our management software

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:42.240
for Velostrata.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.540
So install that
through Marketplace.

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:46.310
You install it as a
VM running, and you

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:48.230
connect to your on-prem.

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.360
Between the two,
we assume that you

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:54.900
have IP connectivity between
your on-premise environment

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:56.510
and your GCP cluster.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.660
That can be achieved by a VPC.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:02.160
That can be achieved
by an interconnect.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.940
But you have to have IP
connectivity between your two

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:08.200
environments.

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:13.710
We create, then, a local
cache on the cloud.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:17.340
And what we do is--

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:18.600
now, listen to this.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:23.340
We have a VM running, and
we started the migration.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:28.310
In 5 to 10 minutes, depending
if it's Linux or Windows,

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:32.570
your new environment will be
up and running on the cloud.

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:36.470
The way we do it is,
we start the new VM

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:38.460
or the new container,
so I'm talking

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:41.730
of two use cases, either the
VM to VM or VM to container.

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:45.590
We fire up the new
container, for example.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:49.850
The data initially is still
residing on your on premise,

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:55.250
and is being streamed
over your IP connection

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:57.960
to the new environment.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:01.760
And that data is
getting cached locally.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:06.260
The cache grows, and you can
start testing your application.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:08.400
You can start using
it right away.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:12.560
You don't need to wait for
all the data, all the disk,

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:15.890
to be migrated for you to
know if your application will

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:18.710
start working on the cloud.

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:22.250
And we achieve that with
our streaming Anthos Migrate

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.350
technology.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:27.080
And you benefit
from that technology

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:31.010
whether you do VM to VM
migration or VM to containers

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.280
migration.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:36.620
If things don't go
well, you can roll back.

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:39.080
We also have ways for
you to do testing.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:42.500
So if you are running
production in your environment,

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:45.970
we can actually
make a copy and get

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:50.720
you to test a clone of
that VM on the cloud,

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:52.330
so it's less disruptive.

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:56.170
And then we give you the way
to migrate at [INAUDIBLE] later

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:57.580
on production environments.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:01.150
So if you want to migrate
hundreds, thousands of VMs,

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:04.869
you can do that on a schedule
with automation tools

00:17:04.869 --> 00:17:06.560
that we provide.

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:10.480
So that's how the
whole migration

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:11.710
is working under the hood.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:20.250
Next, now that you
are with containers,

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:23.970
for example, where
do you see your logs?

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:26.609
So on your VM,
initially you go and you

00:17:26.609 --> 00:17:29.580
have to look at log files
and understand error messages

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:30.840
and so forth.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:40.010
We have integrated the logs from
your VMs with Stackdriver with

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:43.320
the GKE Dashboard and,
more importantly--

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.360
and David will be
telling you about this--

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:48.710
with service management.

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:51.830
And so you basically
can see your logs

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:54.850
as you would see logs from--

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:58.980
you see your log outputs here
in the Stackdriver output.

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:01.070
And so you can
interact with APIs,

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:04.040
with monitoring management
tools if you have,

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:09.140
but it is a modern
GCP native display.

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:11.470
Same goes with
the GKE dashboard.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:12.770
OK.

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:16.048
You can see what your app is
doing, how much CPU it's using,

00:18:16.048 --> 00:18:16.590
and so forth.

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:23.810
Now, the next point
is, well, what

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:26.030
can you migrate to containers?

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:28.340
What really can
you do if you are

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:31.160
ready to migrate your
VM to containers?

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:37.060
What we're doing is, basically,
Linux operating systems today.

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:39.070
We don't support
yet Windows, Windows

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:41.873
because we don't want you to
have Windows on containers.

00:18:41.873 --> 00:18:42.790
So it has to be Linux.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:46.120
It has to be Red Hat,
CentOS, SUSE, Ubuntu--

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:48.490
those are the main
operating systems--

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:51.280
Tomcat, Apache,
Websphere, Weblogic,

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:56.890
JBoss type of middleware
applications, databases.

00:18:56.890 --> 00:19:00.400
So all of these legacy
type of stateful workloads

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:03.190
you can migrate to GKE.

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:04.850
Certain things that
we don't yet do

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:10.510
because, what we announced was
our beta that we're working

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:12.250
on now on this product,
we don't want you

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.500
get to move production.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:16.900
We don't want you to
move sensitive data

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:22.610
because we're on a private
beta at the moment.

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:26.830
And the other thing we don't do
is high performance databases,

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:31.380
such as Hadoop, big
data type of workloads.

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:35.020
And here, for
transparency purposes,

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:37.900
we've outlined the things we
support, we don't support.

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:42.750
Keep in mind that the product is
evolving, roadmap and so forth.

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:45.070
So if there are things
that you'd like to get,

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:46.720
come talk to us at the booths.

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:47.990
Come meet us today.

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:52.210
Talk to your field
representatives,

00:19:52.210 --> 00:19:54.590
and we can work with you
to prioritize things.

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:56.353
This is just to
give you a framework

00:19:56.353 --> 00:19:58.270
so you can understand
if it's something that's

00:19:58.270 --> 00:19:59.510
applicable to you right away.

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:07.180
With that, I'd like to
give you a little demo,

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:10.550
just so you can
visualize the product.

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:14.170
So let's go ahead and try
to play this recording.

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:16.780
So while this loads I
just want to prepare you.

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:18.940
It's a CRM application.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:25.270
So it's basically going to be
a web app that has the database

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:27.310
and customer base CRM.

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:29.320
And it's going to be two VMs.

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:31.750
So I have it running as two
different virtual machines

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:32.290
on VMware.

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:35.500
One is a database, and one is
the web server in application.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:40.870
And we'll migrate these two
VMs from on-premise to GKE.

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:41.590
OK.

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:44.860
And here we're
going to play this.

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:46.720
And we can spend a
lot more time with you

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:48.400
if you stop by our booth.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.080
We can show you the demo live.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:53.000
Here, for purpose of
time and everything,

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:57.220
I just want to play a recording
so it just gets things going,

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:58.680
so you see the concepts.

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:01.480
But then we can spend more
time on the live demo.

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:04.060
So as you see, we have,
as I said, two VMs.

00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:08.020
One is running on CentOS and the
other one is running on SUSE.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.720
So I have two different
operating systems as a start,

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.750
and that's my CRM app
running on premise.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:15.430
So two VMs, two OSs.

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:16.760
I can log in.

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:19.150
And what you see here when
I log in on the local IP

00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:21.220
is the look of the app.

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:23.270
I have a GKE
cluster called Demo,

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:26.050
which is running a
specific version of GKE.

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:29.980
And I have, basically,
a YAML file.

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:34.240
Remember, I told you that we
started the migration from YAML

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:38.000
from Kubernetes, so we have
a file where we say, look,

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:39.820
this is the VM number.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:43.510
We want to use streaming for
our Velostrata technology,

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:46.360
and we want to do, here,
a test plan, for example.

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:50.740
We point to a persistent disk,
and we define the app name

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:53.770
and the way it will
look once it's migrated

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:58.280
on the destination environment.

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:01.390
And so we're going to go ahead
and execute the YAML file.

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:03.430
It's at the very bottom here.

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:05.380
We do a kubectl apply.

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:07.390
And basically what
you see, we refresh

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:12.100
Services and Workloads,
here is basically now two--

00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:16.120
you can see the two
nodes, app and database,

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:17.470
and you can see the services.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:20.620
We have a load bouncer with
a local IP on the cloud,

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:23.410
and we login onto that
IP, and it's the same app.

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:27.910
Now that app has moved and is
running as containers on GKE.

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:30.960
Then we go in and we
enable Istio as an add-on

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:33.130
onto our cluster.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:36.240
Now when we do that, basically,
we can go in Stackdriver,

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:37.750
and we can see data.

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:40.050
We can see the graphs.

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.540
And a lot more
things we can do is

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:45.430
going to be what David is going
to talk to you about next,

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:50.040
which is service mesh, which
will be added functions of what

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:53.250
you do once you are
on GKE and you've

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:56.350
migrated our application.

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:59.550
So with that, Dave, I'd like
to call you up and take it away

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:00.240
for us.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:03.170
And I'll put it back
in presentation mode.

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.480
DAVID MUNRO: So first of
all, thank you Lucien.

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:07.883
So I just actually want
to recap because one

00:23:07.883 --> 00:23:09.800
of the things that's
really amazing there is--

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:12.650
I don't know whether you just
realized what you've witnessed,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:16.700
but you literally
witnessed a VM application

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:22.160
on premise migrating
live, directly to GKE.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:24.562
So I think that's actually
pretty amazing, personally.

00:23:24.562 --> 00:23:26.520
I think that actually
gives you an opportunity,

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:27.937
and this is something
that you can

00:23:27.937 --> 00:23:30.010
do while your
applications are running.

00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:31.940
So I think that's actually
a pretty big thing.

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:37.970
When Lucien asked before
around the number of people

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:40.970
who understand or
know what GKE is,

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:42.960
it was a very, very
large number of people.

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:47.120
So the other thing that we
talk about a lot from a Google

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:49.490
perspective is Istio
and service mesh.

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:51.900
So Istio is an
open-source project.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:55.310
It was founded primarily
by IBM and Google.

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:57.530
There is a large number
of contributors now--

00:23:57.530 --> 00:24:00.290
Cisco, VMware, Red Hat.

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.840
There are a large number of
people contributing to Istio,

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:05.118
and we're getting a lot
of community support.

00:24:05.118 --> 00:24:07.160
So this is actually
something that we're actually

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:09.170
investing quite heavily in.

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:12.350
And we think this is a natural
extension for both Kubernetes

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.390
and non-Kubernetes environments.

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:17.630
But also, it gives
you the ability

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:20.690
to do things from a service
management perspective

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:23.390
that you may not necessarily--

00:24:23.390 --> 00:24:26.410
it reduces the need for you
to instrument your code,

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:28.160
and you can take
advantage of those things

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:29.815
effectively out of the box.

00:24:29.815 --> 00:24:31.190
So one of the
things that we talk

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:35.630
about would be, let's do
the migration that you just

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:42.350
saw but do that into a service
mesh enabled GKE cluster.

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:44.510
By doing that, you
can actually take

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:47.540
advantage of some of the
service management capabilities

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:51.270
that you get from a
service mesh from day one.

00:24:51.270 --> 00:24:54.360
So there are three main
tenants around service mesh.

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:57.020
So if I look at the
three key benefits

00:24:57.020 --> 00:25:00.110
that service mesh
provide, it's effectively

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:02.390
uniform observability.

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:05.790
It is operational agility.

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:07.730
This is the ability
to migrate traffic,

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:11.550
do circuit breaking, retries
within your environment,

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:14.010
and then policy-driven security.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.340
So this is from a
microservices environment.

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:21.080
All your nice, freshly migrated
VM workloads into containers,

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:24.500
you can start applying these
tenants straight away out

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:25.907
of the box.

00:25:25.907 --> 00:25:27.740
So let's drill into
these just a little bit,

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.350
and then I'll do a little bit
of a demonstration on service

00:25:30.350 --> 00:25:32.780
management.

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:34.930
So one of the things that
service mesh provides

00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:38.890
is it provides uniform
metrics and flow logs

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:41.190
across your entire environment.

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:45.000
When we deploy a
container into Kubernetes

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:49.740
we use a Istio sidecar
injector to basically

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:54.090
automatically inject a sidecar
co-located in every pod

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:55.920
associated with that service.

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:59.880
The advantage of doing that
is, in a language agnostic way,

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:03.330
I now get a very consistent
set of metrics and flow

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:05.730
logs through my
entire environment.

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:07.800
I can use them as
golden signals.

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:10.710
I can use them to get an
understanding of these service

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:14.460
dependencies that I have
within my environment.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:16.530
These service
dependencies can literally

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:22.410
give you a way to understand
how your services are connected.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:25.950
And this is actually based
on the traffic flows that

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:28.260
is going through your mesh.

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:30.330
The last thing
you can do is, now

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:33.870
I have these really, really
nice uniformed consistent flow

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:37.530
logs and metrics, I can use
them as my golden signals

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:40.830
to set up SLOs on
those services.

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:45.000
I can look at the
service dependencies

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:49.170
to understand what my critical
customer user journey is

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:52.200
with respect to what's
going to impact my end

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:55.110
SLA to my customers.

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:01.090
The other key thing that
we actually look at here,

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:04.780
within a service mesh, is
the ability to add agility.

00:27:04.780 --> 00:27:06.470
So there's two things here.

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:10.990
One here is around the ability
to shift traffic or split

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:11.860
traffic.

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:13.930
Canary would be a
good example of this.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:16.450
I can move, based on a
weighting, a certain amount

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:19.420
of traffic from one
service to a newer version

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:21.520
of that service [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:23.830
There was a talk
earlier this week

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:29.200
where a customer using
Istio has increased

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:33.290
the number of roll-outs
from around one a day

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:36.210
to on target to
15,000 this year.

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:38.460
And the main reason they're
able to do that is they're

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:41.770
now incrementally releasing
multiple times a day

00:27:41.770 --> 00:27:43.300
in very small increments.

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:44.840
That's doing two things.

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:46.360
That is actually
reducing the risk

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:48.010
because every change
is incremental,

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:50.530
and they've got very
fine-grained controls on how

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:52.390
they canary those releases.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:55.000
This is shortening
the time it takes

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.690
for them to get their changes
out to their customers, which

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.860
is really important.

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:02.590
The other key thing
that Istio provides

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:06.070
is the ability to do things
like circuit breaking.

00:28:06.070 --> 00:28:07.810
I can do retries.

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:10.430
I can build this into the
infrastructure itself.

00:28:10.430 --> 00:28:12.910
So if my VM application
that I've just

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.190
migrated to
containers didn't have

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:17.320
any of that sort of
capability built into it,

00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:19.600
I can now start picking
up these capabilities just

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:21.640
from the service
mesh infrastructure.

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:27.660
The last thing is
around security.

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:33.610
So with Istio or service
mesh, I can effectively

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:39.270
apply a identity-based security
model between my services.

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:41.870
So I can migrate my
services, and I can actually

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:43.490
pick that up straight away.

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:46.370
I can also start looking
at that interconnect

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:48.440
and start getting
security insights.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:52.078
So let's just take two minutes
and have a quick look at what

00:28:52.078 --> 00:28:53.120
that actually looks like.

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:58.760
So I actually have a
microservice application

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:00.680
running.

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:02.660
And these are the
services that are actually

00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:06.920
operational within
the actual mesh.

00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:09.200
Let me just make that--

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:10.860
can everyone see
that OK or do I need

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:12.075
to make a little bit bigger?

00:29:12.075 --> 00:29:12.930
It's OK?

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:14.040
All right.

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:17.190
So what I can actually do
here is I can go through,

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:21.840
and I can quickly look at the
actual topology associated

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:25.570
with this particular
environment.

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:27.330
So I understand the
service dependencies.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:29.520
I can highlight a
specific service

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:31.620
and understand the
actual traffic that's

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:34.250
traversing this environment.

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:36.260
If I was to click
on that service,

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:39.500
I can go to the
service dashboard

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:41.450
and look at that
particular service.

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:43.890
You'll notice, on this
particular service,

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:46.970
I have not set any SLOs.

00:29:46.970 --> 00:29:48.890
One of the things
you'll also notice

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:54.090
is I automatically have
security recommendation.

00:29:54.090 --> 00:30:00.360
So then this particular service,
I have not enabled MTLS.

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:03.180
The flow logs and metrics has
given me that information,

00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:05.070
and it's giving me
now a recommendation

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:07.230
of how I can
increase the security

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:08.925
posture of my environment.

00:30:08.925 --> 00:30:10.800
So these are the things
that the service mesh

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.120
can provide for you
straight out of the box.

00:30:13.120 --> 00:30:15.210
And this can now
be applied directly

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:18.060
to those VMs workloads that
have been migrated directly

00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:20.830
into GKE.

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:24.100
I get very consistent,
these nice flow

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:28.030
logs and metrics associated
with the services

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:30.190
that I've now just migrated.

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:34.930
I can start doing things like
set SLOs on these services.

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:38.860
So setting an SLO, very simple.

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:41.530
Pushing through, selecting,
say, availability

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:43.240
would be an example SLO.

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:46.150
95.4.

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:48.103
Actually, that would
be pretty impressive.

00:30:51.890 --> 00:30:55.720
You can actually do this based
on a rolling period of time

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:58.600
or a calendar period
of time, depending

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:01.600
on whether you're setting
something up more for internal

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:04.510
or more to match a
business objective.

00:31:04.510 --> 00:31:07.780
Windowing can also be
optionally added with respect

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:12.910
to applying now an
alert associated

00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:15.310
with a smaller period of time.

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:19.750
And it would literally just
be as simple as applying that,

00:31:19.750 --> 00:31:21.870
and now I actually
have an SLO set

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:23.830
for that particular service.

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:25.890
So that's just kind
of a little bit of a--

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:28.680
if you think about, again,
what Lucien demonstrated,

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:32.190
he migrated VM workloads
straight to Kubernetes.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:35.370
If you migrate those
VM-based workloads

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:37.110
into a Kubernetes
cluster that has

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:40.920
service management or
a service mesh enabled,

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:44.550
this then just effectively
just works out of the box.

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:47.250
So at this point, I'd
actually like to pause

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:50.520
and invite Leon up to
talk a little bit more

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:58.480
in technical detail
around Anthos Migrate.

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:00.490
And I will hand it over.

00:32:00.490 --> 00:32:00.993
Thank you.

00:32:00.993 --> 00:32:02.410
LEONID VASETSKY:
Thank you, David.

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:11.190
So I'd like to
give you a glimpse

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:16.680
of inner insights of how it
works, where we're heading.

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:18.720
So this is a very young product.

00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:20.040
So we are just starting.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:21.640
It's a long journey.

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:24.177
And I want to show you
what we're doing now

00:32:24.177 --> 00:32:25.260
and what we're doing next.

00:32:28.635 --> 00:32:37.840
But before that, I'd like
to give you a little bit

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:40.630
of history, back to
the history lane.

00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:46.260
So let's think of the developer,
the application developer that

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:50.050
work on application five
years ago, maybe 10 years ago.

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:50.550
Right.

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:53.920
So he was writing
code, and then he

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:57.190
wanted to run it in an
[INAUDIBLE] way, probably

00:32:57.190 --> 00:33:00.790
bundled with contained some
runtime, like Java or Python

00:33:00.790 --> 00:33:01.840
maybe.

00:33:01.840 --> 00:33:04.030
And he wanted to
put it somewhere.

00:33:04.030 --> 00:33:08.890
And the best place to put it,
in [INAUDIBLE] way, was VM.

00:33:08.890 --> 00:33:11.900
And this was convenient,
powered by VMware

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:16.630
was extremely popular, so every
application that you write

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:23.360
ended up being with this
stack on the left side.

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:26.350
And you only need application
maybe two or three services

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:30.220
around it, but you were getting
the virtual hardware that you

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:35.350
need to manage, and network,
and disks, and operating system

00:33:35.350 --> 00:33:39.400
with kernel and staff, and all
the infrastructure that you

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:43.060
need to accommodate, like
logs, and security policies,

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:45.380
and users that can access it.

00:33:45.380 --> 00:33:48.220
So all this is a good start.

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:51.090
But then you're starting
to get more of those.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:53.790
And specifically, the painful
that those application

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:59.800
[INAUDIBLE] mentioned, small or
mid-sized stateful application,

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:05.200
maybe simple database,
web, middle-tier apps,

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:06.670
they started accumulated.

00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:11.350
This can be within apps
for some on some enterprise

00:34:11.350 --> 00:34:16.770
or maybe off-the-shelf
applications that you managed.

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:20.650
And this is very, very painful
and become very, very painful.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:22.929
Now imagine, today, you
are a container developer.

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:25.780
So you can develop your
application packages

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:28.780
in a Docker container and
run it with Kubernetes.

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:30.550
So today, if you
develop a new one,

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:32.810
you have a way of doing that.

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:35.889
But what will you do
with all this leftovers?

00:34:35.889 --> 00:34:38.800
And this is a very,
very painful rewrite.

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:44.330
So what we wanted to do is
a phased approach or phased

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:47.170
journey for those
legacy applications

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:52.120
to get to the modern
way, and they're not

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:53.739
number of stops in the middle.

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:56.590
And these stops can
help you benefit

00:34:56.590 --> 00:35:00.970
from some of the ecosystem,
maybe not all of this,

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:04.090
and then you can benefit
from more and more.

00:35:04.090 --> 00:35:06.160
So the first one,
the first stop,

00:35:06.160 --> 00:35:09.820
the one that you all
actually saw in the demo

00:35:09.820 --> 00:35:14.230
that we, to the application,
we didn't change anything

00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:18.490
for this application, but we
peeled away all the low level

00:35:18.490 --> 00:35:22.690
stuff that is not required for
this application to function.

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:27.580
It's required, but it's not must
to be the same stuff in the VM.

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.330
So we took away OS
kernel and drivers.

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:35.110
Now it's provided by a container
as orchestration framework.

00:35:35.110 --> 00:35:36.650
We took away logging.

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:38.770
We took away networking.

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:41.550
Networking is now
handled by Kubernetes.

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:43.210
We took away storage.

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:48.220
And we leave application and
the services that you may have,

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:53.220
maybe some cron jobs for
this application to run.

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:54.860
So this is the first step.

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:57.680
It allows you to
get into the stage

00:35:57.680 --> 00:35:59.690
when you manage the
Kubernetes, leverage all

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:03.020
these good features
that you have,

00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:07.830
but it's still not the
end of the journey.

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:12.500
The next bar is going
to this stage, where

00:36:12.500 --> 00:36:16.790
we want to take this upper part
of the VM and split it further.

00:36:16.790 --> 00:36:19.490
Maybe some application
will be rewritten,

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:21.200
some of our application
will be just

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:24.590
breaking down to the
application and the data.

00:36:24.590 --> 00:36:27.200
And maybe some
application will be

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:32.930
left as is because
you don't find it cost

00:36:32.930 --> 00:36:35.880
effective to invest in them.

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:38.460
And so this is the
journey that we follow.

00:36:38.460 --> 00:36:42.540
And the beta will
cover mostly this far,

00:36:42.540 --> 00:36:47.940
then this part will come preview
right pretty much after that.

00:36:50.810 --> 00:36:52.460
So this is one aspect.

00:36:52.460 --> 00:36:56.020
The second aspect, which
is also very important,

00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:58.430
is how you're
doing your journey.

00:36:58.430 --> 00:37:01.630
So we focused in all the
demo and announcements,

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:08.820
we focused on getting workloads
from VMware to GKE, right?

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:10.890
But basically there
are two dimensions

00:37:10.890 --> 00:37:12.900
that you need to handle.

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:16.080
One is migrating things
from remote location

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:19.410
to another location, can be
either cloud, can be VMware,

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:22.510
again, on-prem data center.

00:37:22.510 --> 00:37:24.880
And maybe you don't
need to migrate at all.

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:27.622
If you're moving from
VMware to GKE On-Prem

00:37:27.622 --> 00:37:29.080
you don't need to
migrate the data.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:31.090
The data is already there.

00:37:31.090 --> 00:37:32.950
So there's dimension number one.

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:39.280
Dimension number two is
basically moving from the VM

00:37:39.280 --> 00:37:41.090
to the container.

00:37:41.090 --> 00:37:44.120
So we build product in a
way, and built architecture

00:37:44.120 --> 00:37:47.420
in a way, that you can do
any of those transitions

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:49.080
independently.

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:54.380
We can get you, of course,
the quick way, this one,

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:58.880
when you move from
VMware to GKE and cloud,

00:37:58.880 --> 00:37:59.930
getting all the benefits.

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:02.210
But you can go either way.

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:05.450
You can first most
GCE VM and then

00:38:05.450 --> 00:38:09.150
decide to containerize
this VM and so on.

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:14.330
And this is very important
in that both of those

00:38:14.330 --> 00:38:15.570
are handled by the product.

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:18.960
And it's different types and
different places on the road

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.420
map when we go there.

00:38:21.420 --> 00:38:23.210
But this is
important flexibility

00:38:23.210 --> 00:38:26.150
that you want to do.

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:34.550
And especially with Kubernetes,
when you have the storage

00:38:34.550 --> 00:38:35.150
obstructions.

00:38:35.150 --> 00:38:38.690
So from Kubernetes perspective,
you consuming storage.

00:38:38.690 --> 00:38:41.735
And storage can be
remote or can be local.

00:38:41.735 --> 00:38:43.610
If you're consuming
storage, and then running

00:38:43.610 --> 00:38:46.430
your workload this
container, it doesn't matter.

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:49.880
You want to use this
Kubernetes obstructions

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:52.150
to hide the storage location.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:58.090
Obviously, this is just
a recap, small recap,

00:38:58.090 --> 00:39:04.580
what happened in the demo,
just to give you a replay.

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:09.300
So Lucien [INAUDIBLE]
presented a corporate user

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:13.020
starting with the VMs on-prem
and connection to them.

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:19.230
Then we moved those VM to
being in containers in GKE.

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:22.160
We started to use
networking of Kubernetes.

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:26.400
So at this point, application
were connecting to the database

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:31.920
by its DNS name and the service,
and right now it's both,

00:39:31.920 --> 00:39:33.570
so it can any IP.

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:35.440
You don't need to
manage IP anymore.

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:39.000
You have a load balancer
to get you outside access.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:44.100
And then once those
are containers,

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:46.860
you can see the logs, you
can see all the benefits

00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:52.470
from the Istio, and other
things that David show you.

00:39:52.470 --> 00:39:57.270
One thing that Lucien
mentioned as well

00:39:57.270 --> 00:40:03.350
is that we do have a technology
a couple of years now,

00:40:03.350 --> 00:40:08.240
when we want to, we can migrate
storage from remote locations

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:10.010
to cloud.

00:40:10.010 --> 00:40:11.990
And we want to leverage
this technology

00:40:11.990 --> 00:40:15.470
when we move storage
for containers

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:17.790
and when we move
VMs to containers.

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:21.200
So we have the
same infrastructure

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:24.830
that can bring storage
from remote locations

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:28.100
to their workloads,
no matter if workloads

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:30.500
are VMs or containers.

00:40:30.500 --> 00:40:32.660
And this is how the
product is built.

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:38.270
And again, you can use it
to migrate first, VM as VM,

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:42.350
and then containerized, or
you can go directly to that.

00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:44.340
We're talking a
lot about storage.

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:46.260
Why is it so important?

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:49.670
This is where caching,
and latency optimizations,

00:40:49.670 --> 00:40:52.490
and deduplication-- all
the things that the storage

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:55.910
layers gives you, Velostrata
storage layer gives you,

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:57.000
they come into play.

00:40:57.000 --> 00:40:58.910
And it's the same
optimization that we

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:02.510
use for containers and the VMs.

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:07.130
And this is basically what
gives you the ability to test

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:10.910
something or to run something
in couple of minutes because you

00:41:10.910 --> 00:41:13.970
cannot move terabytes of
data in a couple of minutes.

00:41:18.100 --> 00:41:25.130
So let's get another
level down of how this

00:41:25.130 --> 00:41:26.430
works on the Kubernetes side.

00:41:26.430 --> 00:41:31.200
So quick show of hands,
who is familiar with CSI?

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:31.700
That's less.

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:33.760
So I won't go to the CSI here.

00:41:33.760 --> 00:41:35.860
It's a lots of
lectures about that.

00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:38.920
CSI is container storage
interface, something that

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:41.620
became GA in Kubernetes 113.

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:44.560
Basically, this is
one of the things that

00:41:44.560 --> 00:41:48.310
allows you to obstruct
the storage implementation

00:41:48.310 --> 00:41:50.590
from the orchestration.

00:41:50.590 --> 00:41:55.900
So Kubernetes will orchestrate
the workload creation

00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:58.810
and will orchestrate
the volume provisioning,

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:00.400
volume attachment.

00:42:00.400 --> 00:42:04.360
And then you will
be, as a driver,

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:06.790
as the provider of
the storage, you

00:42:06.790 --> 00:42:09.980
will be providing the
implementation for that.

00:42:09.980 --> 00:42:11.440
So what will happen?

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:16.930
When you deploy the [INAUDIBLE]
that Lucien showed you, you

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:20.900
actually trigger the activity
from Kubernetes, which says,

00:42:20.900 --> 00:42:24.170
OK, I need to schedule
this pod on this node,

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:26.800
and I need to make
sure that this node has

00:42:26.800 --> 00:42:28.420
access to storage.

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:30.560
And you ask me for the
storage of this type,

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:31.910
so I need provisioning.

00:42:31.910 --> 00:42:36.010
So at this point, Kubernetes
goes to the storage driver

00:42:36.010 --> 00:42:39.340
and ask me, OK, look, I need
to provision the storage.

00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:41.560
This can be native
storage driver.

00:42:41.560 --> 00:42:43.090
For some storage
vendor it can be

00:42:43.090 --> 00:42:46.720
native storage driver
for GCE persistent disk

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:49.300
or, in our case
of the streaming,

00:42:49.300 --> 00:42:51.250
Velostrata Control Plane.

00:42:51.250 --> 00:42:54.670
It basically says, look, if I
have a needed provision volume,

00:42:54.670 --> 00:43:00.120
I'm exposing the storage of
this VM from on-premise or other

00:43:00.120 --> 00:43:03.210
[INAUDIBLE] to this cluster.

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:07.230
So Kubernetes is happy,
volume is provisioned,

00:43:07.230 --> 00:43:14.550
now he decide which node
should be used to run pod,

00:43:14.550 --> 00:43:16.690
and then it's come
in attachment.

00:43:16.690 --> 00:43:23.770
So he connects this node
to the storage backend,

00:43:23.770 --> 00:43:25.650
mounts relevant volumes.

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:29.700
And at this point,
the workload that runs

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:34.290
consumed this storage, again,
being it remote or local.

00:43:34.290 --> 00:43:37.230
What's important in that
system, so it's fully integrated

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:38.520
with Kubernetes obstruction.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:43.530
It means that if you want
just to migrate the storage,

00:43:43.530 --> 00:43:46.950
and you don't want
to migrate your VM,

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:54.230
just maybe you already have
MySQL container, MySQL image

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:58.560
that you want to use, you
only want to move the data,

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:00.110
then you will do the same.

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:03.380
So you will attach the storage
to the existing container,

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:10.790
and it will benefit from the
same streaming and compression

00:44:10.790 --> 00:44:13.980
technology.

00:44:13.980 --> 00:44:16.960
And last, what you can
also do with the storage--

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:20.130
and this is something
that we're also providing.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:25.980
This is what we call optimized
data, data extraction,

00:44:25.980 --> 00:44:27.490
or selective data extraction.

00:44:27.490 --> 00:44:31.740
So we provide the job in
the Kubernetes cluster--

00:44:31.740 --> 00:44:33.510
again, fully managed
by Kubernetes,

00:44:33.510 --> 00:44:36.300
fully obstructed by
Kubernetes obstruction-- that

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:38.970
can take you're
streaming storage

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:43.110
and extract part of the files of
part of the data that you want.

00:44:43.110 --> 00:44:48.510
For example, you only want
your MySQL database data.

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:53.300
So this will fetch this
storage in a streaming manner

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:55.890
inefficient with all
the optimization,

00:44:55.890 --> 00:45:01.450
and it will deploy it to the
storage of your choosing.

00:45:01.450 --> 00:45:03.010
It can be native
cloud storage, it

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:07.750
can be storage vendor
that runs in the cloud.

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:12.560
And this is also a foundation
of what we call the next phase.

00:45:12.560 --> 00:45:14.580
So this is the same
technology that

00:45:14.580 --> 00:45:17.620
will then be used to
extract the application.

00:45:17.620 --> 00:45:20.670
So let's say you have
multiple services running

00:45:20.670 --> 00:45:24.870
on the same VM, and you want
to extract some of them,

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:27.960
so we'll extract part of
the data will go here,

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:30.130
part of the data will go there.

00:45:30.130 --> 00:45:33.090
And this will be the same
component doing this job.

00:45:36.230 --> 00:45:40.430
So with that, I'll heading back
to Lucien for a quick wrap-up.

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:45.070
LUCIEN AVRAMOV:
Thank you, Leonid.

00:45:45.070 --> 00:45:48.910
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:48.910 --> 00:45:51.130
So what did we learn?

00:45:51.130 --> 00:45:54.300
Well, hopefully, I hope that
you understand, first of all,

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:58.200
that modernization is no
longer something where

00:45:58.200 --> 00:46:00.890
it means you have to rewrite
everything from scratch.

00:46:00.890 --> 00:46:03.067
And it means that if
you want to go there,

00:46:03.067 --> 00:46:04.650
we're here to help
you on this journey

00:46:04.650 --> 00:46:08.270
with moving those application
on a modern environment.

00:46:08.270 --> 00:46:12.480
Two, I hope you understand
that state stateful apps can

00:46:12.480 --> 00:46:18.030
actually run now in GKE without
any need for writing them

00:46:18.030 --> 00:46:18.840
again.

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:21.050
It's kind of the same
as the first point.

00:46:21.050 --> 00:46:24.990
And then number three,
this can be directly done

00:46:24.990 --> 00:46:28.710
from on premise to
the cloud, and you can

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:30.370
be up and running in minutes.

00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:35.430
Now, one thing to
remember is we don't

00:46:35.430 --> 00:46:38.650
have to migrate you directly
to containers and to the cloud

00:46:38.650 --> 00:46:39.720
if you're on premise.

00:46:39.720 --> 00:46:41.470
We can do it in two steps.

00:46:41.470 --> 00:46:45.570
We can move you to the cloud
on a VM, get you comfortable,

00:46:45.570 --> 00:46:48.860
and then move that
VM to a container.

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:49.440
OK.

00:46:49.440 --> 00:46:51.480
So we can meet you
in your journey

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:53.220
where you are, and
this comes back

00:46:53.220 --> 00:46:55.050
to some of the questions
on the Dory today

00:46:55.050 --> 00:46:57.990
where one question was,
well, how about Windows?

00:46:57.990 --> 00:47:00.780
Can I use Anthos
Migrate for Windows?

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:04.200
Well, what we can do today
with Anthos Migrate for Windows

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:06.270
is VM-to-VM migration.

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:09.390
So we can get you to the
cloud running as a VM,

00:47:09.390 --> 00:47:12.960
and when we have support
for Windows on GKE--

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:14.640
hopefully later this year--

00:47:14.640 --> 00:47:18.240
then we can migrate that
VM into a container.

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:21.060
Number two, another question
on the Dory was, well,

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:24.270
is Velostrata a requirement
of Anthos Migrate?

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:26.410
Anthos Migrate is Velostrata.

00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:28.140
It's the same thing.

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:31.350
You install Velostrata, and
you can do VM-to-VM migration,

00:47:31.350 --> 00:47:34.590
and now you can do
VM to GKE migration.

00:47:34.590 --> 00:47:37.020
So hopefully that clears it up.

